# The USB stick (FAT32, label TITANOS) holds the boot files, the system slots
# and /config. FAT has no permissions, so everything on it is root-only.
#
# The stick is mounted on first use (titand reads /config when it starts)
# and then stays mounted until shutdown: an automount without idle timeout.
# Mounting at boot instead failed when the stick showed up later than the
# device timeout (slow USB controllers, QEMU); the automount waits for it
# at first access, when it is there. FAT has no journal: flush writes every
# change through at once, so a power cut or a pulled stick loses at most
# the write in progress. The last field lets systemd-fsck check the stick
# once per boot, before it is first mounted, and clear the dirty bit left
# behind.
LABEL=TITANOS  /efi  vfat  rw,umask=0077,flush,noauto,x-systemd.automount,x-systemd.device-timeout=10s  0  2

# The root of the NFS tree. titand bind-mounts the folder of every NFS share
# into it. A tmpfs of its own, because the overlay of / cannot be exported.
tmpfs  /srv/nfs  tmpfs  size=1m,mode=0755,nosuid,nodev  0  0
